meta {
  name: 01-Login
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{host}}/api/auth/login
  body: json
  auth: none
}

body:json {
  {
    "email": "admin@dreamhomes.local",
    "password": "AuditAdmin#1!"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
}

script:post-response {
  if (res && res.body && res.body.token) {
    bru.setVar("jwt", res.body.token);
  }
}

docs {
  # 8:42am — laptop open, coffee in hand. First thing every morning.

  Single endpoint, JSON body, returns a token. That part is fine on the
  spec. The spec promises a `LoginResponse { token }` and nothing else —
  no `userId`, no `role`, no `tokenVersion`, no `expiresAt`.

  As an admin operator that already bothers me. If I get a token I can't
  even tell from the response *which account* I just authenticated as. I
  have to make a separate `GET /me` round-trip just to confirm I logged in
  as the admin and not some test account someone forgot about. For a T&S
  console that's wasted clicks every shift change.

  Also: `POST /auth/login` is rate-limited per IP (per the brief). There's
  no documented "admin override" or "internal IP allowlist" — meaning if
  another teammate on the same office NAT is hammering login with a script,
  I get locked out of moderation. That's a real-world problem, not a
  hypothetical.
}
